 ## Background
-The projected tetrahedra method was introduced by Shirley and Tuchman in 1990 as a way to render unstructured volume data using standard graphics hardware [S2, unofficial][S3][S7]. It solved the problem of rendering irregular grids (e.g., from CFD simulations) by avoiding the complexity of ray-tetrahedron intersection tests in software, instead projecting the primitives to screen space and using rasterization [S3][S5]. The method relies on the decomposition of hexahedral cells into tetrahedra to handle regular grids, a technique also used in the Marching Tetrahedrons algorithm for iso-surface extraction [S1, unofficial]. Early implementations faced challenges with visibility sorting, leading to the development of various sorting strategies, including parallelized accurate sorting and sorting-free stochastic methods [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. The family has expanded to include connectivity encoding for compression [S1, unofficial], Delaunay triangulation for mesh generation [S2, unofficial], and integration with neural networks for analytic isosurface extraction [S1, unofficial].
+The use of tetrahedra in volume rendering originated from the need to handle unstructured grids and resolve topological ambiguities in regular grids. The Projected Tetrahedra method was introduced to leverage hardware rasterization for unstructured data [S2, unofficial][S3][S7]. The Marching Tetrahedrons algorithm was developed as an improvement over Marching Cubes to ensure planar faces [S1, unofficial]. Adaptive Delaunay updates are used in modern differentiable rendering frameworks like Radiance Meshes to handle mesh optimization [S1, unofficial]. Grid-based traversal methods were developed to accelerate ray-tetrahedron intersection tests for large unstructured meshes [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Parallel approaches using KD-trees were proposed to distribute the rendering load for large datasets [S1, unofficial]. Connectivity encoding methods like the Cut-Border Machine were developed to compress tetrahedral meshes for transmission [S1, unofficial]. Delaunay triangulation is a standard method for generating tetrahedral meshes from point clouds [S2, unofficial]. TetraSDF is a recent development (2025) that combines neural SDFs with tetrahedral grids for analytic isosurface extraction [S4].
 
